# .gitattributes — byte-exactness enforcement (E10, layer 1 of 3)
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# -------------------
# This repository's product thesis is that a receipt is verifiable byte-for-byte by
# a third party. Every integrity claim it makes — memo `Integrity:` footers, the
# vendored manifest's digest pin, frozen RECEIVED responses — is a digest over exact
# bytes. Git's end-of-line translation is a byte-rewriting tool. It is therefore
# excluded here, globally, for the same reason formatters, linters and auto-fixers
# are excluded from every received-data path.
#
# THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. Measured on 2026-08-21, on this repository's own
# development machine (Git for Windows, `core.autocrlf=true` set in
# C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig — the platform default, not a local choice):
#
#   a cold clone of docs/from_core/Core_to_Canary_Response_001_2026-08-21.md
#   without this file produced 8673 bytes instead of 8533 — 140 CR bytes injected —
#   and scripts/verify_memo.py returned UNVERIFIABLE (exit 3) on a memo that
#   verifies OK (exit 0) in the working tree.
#
# The committed blob was correct in both cases. The corruption happens at checkout.
# That is the trap: the repository looks clean to the author and fails for the
# verifier, which is precisely the failure mode a change-evidence tool cannot have.
#
# `-text` means: never translate line endings, in either direction, on any path.
# It is set for `*` rather than only for the received-data paths, because the
# discipline is not "protect the files we currently remember are byte-sensitive" —
# it is "this repository does not rewrite bytes." A path added later inherits the
# guarantee without anyone having to remember to come back here.
*                       -text

# --- Reinforcement for the paths where a byte change is an integrity failure ---
# These entries are redundant under `* -text` above, and are kept deliberately: they
# state at the path what the global rule states in the abstract, so that anyone
# reading a diff of a received-data directory sees the rule that governs it. If the
# global line is ever weakened, these must survive.

# Inbound memos from core. RECEIVED data: frozen verbatim, never normalised. Their
# `Integrity:` footers are digests over the exact bytes as delivered.
docs/from_core/**       -text

# Outbound memos to core. Sealed with footers computed by scripts/verify_memo.py;
# a byte rewritten after sealing invalidates the seal.
docs/to_core/**         -text

# Vendored rederivable-manifest v3, disk-copied from the forensics repo and pinned by
# vendor/rederivable-manifest.SHA256. Vendored copies are frozen and are never edited
# in place (core R-C001): a byte changed here is indistinguishable from tampering,
# which is the entire point of the digest pin.
vendor/**               -text

# Frozen model replies from the paper-2 cycles. RECEIVED data: byte-for-byte copies of
# upstream artifacts, pinned by fixtures/paper2.SHA256. Reformatting one would change its
# digest and quietly invalidate every measurement taken from it.
fixtures/**             -text

# The C4 incident of record: the complete frozen store from the live phase of 2026-08-22,
# brought under repository custody on 2026-08-27 (Core -> Canary Response 011 §2). RECEIVED
# data in the strongest sense in this repository -- every byte under `runs/` came back from a
# live endpoint, and re-derivation re-classifies verdicts FROM THOSE RAW BYTES rather than
# reading back a recorded verdict. So a formatter or an end-of-line translation here does not
# merely dirty a diff: it changes what the instrument reads, and can flip a verdict in the
# artifact this pillar ships. Pinned by
# docs/evidence/c4-incident-of-record/c4-incident-of-record.SHA256 and checked by
# tests/test_byte_discipline.py. These files are never edited; corrections go in INTEGRITY.md.
docs/evidence/**        -text

# The vendored oneview design spec. RECEIVED data: core's delivery, disk-copied from the
# onedoor repo and byte-pinned by canary/viewer/tokens.py:SPEC_DIGEST. The viewer reads its
# palette OUT OF THIS FILE at import time rather than carrying a transcribed copy, so a byte
# changed here changes what the page is painted with -- and X-6 says a drifted spec raises
# rather than degrading to a bundled fallback. Checked by tests/viewer/test_structure.py.
canary/viewer/_vendor/** -text

# Zip archives carry memos between sessions; the relay is download-and-move only.
*.zip                   -text -diff binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATED, NOT RECEIVED: the one exception to `* -text`, and why it is not one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every rule above protects RECEIVED bytes -- memos, vendored copies, frozen replies,
# the incident of record. Those are evidence: a digest is taken over them and any
# translation destroys it, so git must not touch them.
#
# `docs/launch/onewatch.html` is the opposite kind of file. Nothing is received; it is
# a pure function of the store, regenerated by `python -m canary.viewer`, and the CI
# gate "onewatch regenerates from the store" asserts that the committed page equals a
# fresh generation. Under `* -text` that gate is platform-dependent: the viewer writes
# through Python's text layer, so a Windows generation lands 133 CR bytes that a Linux
# generation does not, and the committed page then differs from a fresh one on any
# machine that is not the author's -- on a repository whose claim is that a stranger
# can recompute what it publishes.
#
# Measured 2026-09-05, the first time this workflow ever ran: committed page 13132
# bytes with 133 CR; fresh generation on Linux 12999 bytes with 0. Identical content.
# (The viewer's own message reports 12962 -- that is the CHARACTER count, and the
# 37-byte gap is UTF-8 multibyte: 25 '·', 3 '—', 3 '…'.)
#
# Normalising here is the repository's existing doctrine applied correctly, not an
# exception to it: received bytes are never rewritten, generated structures are
# canonicalised. The root fix -- having the viewer write LF explicitly rather than
# through the platform's text layer -- is queued; this rule holds regardless of which
# machine runs the generation, which is the property the gate actually needs.
docs/launch/onewatch.html   text eol=lf
